## Exercise: the most important treatment

NICE says **therapeutic exercise** should be offered to everyone with osteoarthritis. This means exercise that is designed to help your joints, such as:

- **strengthening exercises** for the muscles around the joint
- **general activity**, like walking, cycling or swimming
- **stretches** to keep the joint moving

Strong muscles take pressure off the joint. It can take a few weeks to feel the benefit, so keep going. A **physiotherapist** can plan exercises for you. In many areas you can refer yourself to NHS physiotherapy.

## Managing your weight

If you are overweight, losing even a small amount of weight can **ease pain in your knees and hips**, because they carry less load. Your GP can refer you to a weight management service.

## Pain relief

- **Anti-inflammatory gels or creams** (topical NSAIDs, such as ibuprofen gel) are usually tried first for osteoarthritis of the knee, and can help other joints near the skin, like the hands.
- **Anti-inflammatory tablets** (oral NSAIDs) may be offered at the lowest dose for the shortest time, usually with a medicine to protect your stomach. They are not safe for everyone, so talk to your GP or pharmacist.
- **Paracetamol and weak opioids** such as codeine are not usually recommended for long-term use in osteoarthritis. They may help for short periods now and then.
- NICE advises doctors **not to offer strong opioids** for osteoarthritis.

## Injections

A **steroid injection** into the joint may help for a short time during a painful flare, when other treatments have not worked well enough. The effect usually wears off after a few weeks.

## Surgery

A **joint replacement**, most often of the hip or knee, may be offered if your symptoms still have a big effect on your life after trying other treatments. Your age, weight and other health conditions do not rule you out on their own.

## Treatments NICE advises against

- glucosamine supplements
- acupuncture and dry needling
- keyhole surgery to wash out or tidy the knee joint (unless the knee is locking)

{% callout type="good" title="Heat and cold" %}
Many people find a warm bath, a heat pad or a cold pack (wrapped in a towel) eases pain and stiffness. They are cheap and safe to try.
{% /callout %}

